  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Courtside match console",
    "overview": "The prototype uses a dark, quiet reading surface with orange and lime team identity. Strong alignment, prominent scores and an adjacent count-up clock support the operator's next action. History uses ruled records rather than a grid of metric cards. The separate spectator surface gives the score and clock more room.\n\nThis document merges the prototype's own provisional direction with the implemented system in `modules/api/static/style.css`, `operator.html`, `scoreboard.html` and their JavaScript modules. The direction and descriptive language are implementation choices, not a user-selected aesthetic or standing preference. Evidence is code-led: no rendered browser or screenshot inspection is available, visual acceptance is pending, and contrast has not been measured. The scope is the authorized Part 01 manual scoreboard; this document makes no camera, model or automatic-scoring capability claim.\n\nThe original seed considered referee sheets, stadium displays, broadcast desks, fixture boards, club noticeboards and substitution boards. Its retained disciplines are explicit conflicts, consistent team colors, distinct state vocabulary, restraint and readable measurement. Those explorations explain the prototype direction; they do not establish user-approved visual rejections.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Dark reading surface with restrained tonal layering.",
      "Team-colored scores, clearly named actions and written lifecycle states.",
      "Locally served assets and a dedicated numeral face.",
      "Ruled match history that preserves corrections.",
      "Responsive operator controls and a read-only spectator display."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Written State Rule",
        "body": "Pair lifecycle, connectivity and correction color with explicit words; color alone must not carry match state.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Steady Numeral Rule",
        "body": "Use tabular numerals for score, clock and event timestamps. Preserve the local numeral font and keep clock text on one line.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tonal Depth Rule",
        "body": "Continue the existing flat and tonal surface language when extending this prototype; use boundaries and spacing to clarify groups.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** keep team identity consistent across names, numerals and goal actions.",
      "**Do** pair colors with written match, connection and correction states.",
      "**Do** preserve the committed-score update and visible correction history.",
      "**Do** keep fonts and runtime assets locally served so the interface can operate without internet.",
      "**Do** preserve the visible focus outline and reduced-motion behavior.",
      "**Do** inspect empty, connecting, disconnected, ready, live, paused, ended, interrupted, invalid-input, unknown-scorer and undone-goal states before visual acceptance."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** display an uncertain action as a locally confirmed goal.",
      "**Don't** conceal undone records or require known player attribution to show a team goal.",
      "**Don't** add operator write controls to the spectator display.",
      "**Don't** treat this implementation direction as a user-approved aesthetic, verified contrast or completed visual review.",
      "**Don't** imply camera or automatic-scoring capability through decorative live indicators or fabricated match content."
    ]
  }
}
